SUPERSEDED -- DO NOT BUILD OR LOAD. Kept for the record only.

This write-back forces the HQD's CP_HQD_PQ_CONTROL queue-size field back to
the single, un-doubled ring size on GFX7/8 AQL queues. That is the opposite
of what this hardware needs: the doubled size is not an accounting error,
it is the workaround. GFXIP 7/8's CP cannot distinguish a full ring from an
empty one unless the span it masks against is twice the ring, so telling it
the true size re-arms the silent dispatch hang tracked as problem 1 in
RESOLVED_VRAM_MARGINALITY_INVESTIGATION.md.

It appeared to fix something because it was paired with
graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch, which reported a doubled size for a ring
that was never actually mirrored -- so the CP was addressing a span whose
upper half was unmapped, and faulted. Removing the doubling removed the
fault and the workaround together. The real missing piece was
HsaMemFlags.ui32.AQLQueueMemory; see patches/rocm-systems/
aql-ring-queue-full-workaround.patch.

ACTION REQUIRED if this is already installed: the gfx803 box at
192.168.1.214 was running an out-of-tree amdgpu.ko built with this patch as
of 2026-08-28. It has to be rebuilt without it before the ROCR-side fix can
work.

For the record, that ROCR-side fix does not fix the silent dispatch hang
either -- see RESOLVED_VRAM_MARGINALITY_INVESTIGATION.md's top-of-file summary. The hang
is VRAM-clock marginality (mining-tuned VBIOS driving Hynix VRAM above its
rated speed), confirmed on hardware 2026-08-29. This patch's own analysis
of the double-map mechanism is still correct; it just isn't the hang.

Original header follows.

REFERENCE ONLY -- NOT built, applied, or shipped by this repo. Out-of-tree
kernel (amdgpu/amdkfd) driver source, not a userspace ROCm component this
repo's Docker image build touches. Saved here purely as documentation for
the companion userspace patch (patches/rocm-systems/
graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch), whose header explains the full WHY.
Per AGENTS.md's standing rule, driver changes belong outside this repo --
this file exists only so the paired fix is documented next to the
userspace half that depends on it, not as something to `patch -p1` into a
build.

WHY (short version -- see graph-replay-queue-size-cap.patch for the full
investigation): kfd_queue_acquire_buffers() computes the correct halved
ring size needed to validate a GFX7/8 AQL compute queue's VM-mapped
backing buffer ("AQL queues on GFX7 and GFX8 appear twice their actual
size"), but only uses it locally for that one check -- it's never written
back to properties->queue_size, which kfd_mqd_manager_vi.c's update_mqd()
later reads RAW to program the real hardware CP_HQD_PQ_CONTROL queue-size
field and wptr_mask. If userspace reports a doubled ring_size (required to
get hsa_queue_create to succeed above the 64-packet floor at all -- see
the paired patch), that doubled value reaches hardware unmodified,
desyncing the CP's wraparound point from the actual backing buffer and
software's own index masking. Confirmed as a real GPU fault under load
(memory access fault, GPU hang) on real gfx803 hardware without this
write-back; confirmed fixed with it (hsa_queue_create succeeds at the full
131072-packet max, vLLM's HIP-graph-replay repro completes cleanly and
repeatably, no fault).

Verified by building this as a standalone out-of-tree module from
Fedora's kernel-7.1.9-200.fc44 source (matching the box's running
7.1.8-200.fc44 headers -- confirmed safe since the Fedora/RHEL aggregate
patch between those two versions touches zero files under
drivers/gpu/drm/amd; same build recipe as this repo's vllm-gfx803/NOTES.md
documents for the unrelated pqm_debugfs_mqds NULL-deref fix), installing
it over the stock module, and rebooting.

Applies to: drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c, function
kfd_queue_acquire_buffers(). Confirmed present, byte-for-byte identical in
the relevant region, in live mainline Linux at the time of writing -- a
real, unfixed upstream gap, not distro-specific. Worth reporting to
gitlab.freedesktop.org/drm/amd/-/issues.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c
index 0000000000..0000000000 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdkfd/kfd_queue.c
@@ -246,11 +246,23 @@ int kfd_queue_acquire_buffers(struct kfd_process_device *pdd, struct queue_pro
 	/* AQL queues on GFX7 and GFX8 appear twice their actual size */
 	if (properties->type == KFD_QUEUE_TYPE_COMPUTE &&
 	    properties->format == KFD_QUEUE_FORMAT_AQL &&
 	    topo_dev->node_props.gfx_target_version >= 70000 &&
-	    topo_dev->node_props.gfx_target_version < 90000)
+	    topo_dev->node_props.gfx_target_version < 90000) {
 		/* metadata_queue_size not supported on GFX7/GFX8 */
 		expected_queue_size =
 			PAGE_ALIGN(properties->queue_size / 2);
-	else
+		/*
+		 * userspace reports queue_size doubled (matching the VA-size
+		 * check above) so hsa_queue_create can request ring sizes
+		 * beyond the point where PAGE_ALIGN(queue_size/2) no longer
+		 * coincidentally rounds back up to queue_size itself (the
+		 * >1-page case). The MQD/HQD programming path (kfd_mqd_manager_vi.c)
+		 * reads properties->queue_size raw for CP_HQD_PQ_CONTROL's
+		 * queue_size field and wptr_mask -- without this write-back it
+		 * would program hardware with the doubled, not the real, ring
+		 * capacity, desyncing HW wraparound from the actual backing
+		 * buffer. Write back the true (halved) size now that the VA
+		 * check above has validated it against the real allocation.
+		 */
+		properties->queue_size = expected_queue_size;
+	} else
 		expected_queue_size =
 			PAGE_ALIGN(properties->queue_size + properties->metadata_queue_size);
